WEBVTT

00:00.640 --> 00:04.420
Part C. Find all values of
x on the interval negative 4

00:04.420 --> 00:07.670
is less than x is less than
3 for which the graph of g

00:07.670 --> 00:09.580
has a point of inflection.

00:09.580 --> 00:11.460
Give a reason for your answer.

00:11.460 --> 00:15.660
So an inflection
point is a point

00:15.660 --> 00:24.220
where the sign of the
second derivative changes.

00:24.220 --> 00:26.560
So if you take the second
derivative at that point,

00:26.560 --> 00:29.345
or as we go close to that point,
or as we cross that point,

00:29.345 --> 00:30.720
it goes from
positive to negative

00:30.720 --> 00:32.390
or negative to positive.

00:32.390 --> 00:34.110
And to think about
that visually,

00:34.110 --> 00:35.920
you could think
of some examples.

00:35.920 --> 00:42.030
So if you have a curve that
looks something like this,

00:42.030 --> 00:44.820
you'll notice that over
here the slope is negative,

00:44.820 --> 00:46.040
but it's increasing.

00:46.040 --> 00:47.980
It's getting less
negative, less negative.

00:47.980 --> 00:48.740
Then it goes to 0.

00:48.740 --> 00:49.781
Then it keeps increasing.

00:49.781 --> 00:53.110
Slope is increasing, increasing,
all the way to there,

00:53.110 --> 00:55.690
and then it starts
getting less positive.

00:55.690 --> 00:56.720
So it starts decreasing.

00:56.720 --> 00:58.100
So it's increasing.

00:58.100 --> 01:00.739
The slope is increasing over
at this point right over here.

01:00.739 --> 01:02.280
So even though the
slope is negative,

01:02.280 --> 01:03.821
it's getting less
negative over here.

01:03.821 --> 01:04.989
So it's increasing.

01:04.989 --> 01:06.730
And then the slope
keeps increasing.

01:06.730 --> 01:08.480
It keeps on getting
more and more positive

01:08.480 --> 01:09.470
up to about this point.

01:09.470 --> 01:11.261
And then the slope is
positive, but then it

01:11.261 --> 01:12.340
becomes less positive.

01:12.340 --> 01:14.840
So the slope begins
decreasing after that.

01:18.130 --> 01:21.320
So this right over here
is a point of inflection.

01:21.320 --> 01:25.427
The slope has gone from
increasing to decreasing.

01:25.427 --> 01:27.510
And if the other thing
happened, if the slope went

01:27.510 --> 01:28.680
from decreasing to
increasing, that

01:28.680 --> 01:30.180
would also be a
point of inflection.

01:30.180 --> 01:32.710
So if this was maybe some
type of a trigonometric curve,

01:32.710 --> 01:34.450
then you might see
something like this.

01:34.450 --> 01:39.380
And so this also would it
be a point of inflection.

01:39.380 --> 01:43.030
But for this, our g of x is kind
of hard to visualize the way

01:43.030 --> 01:44.702
they've defined it
right over here.

01:44.702 --> 01:46.410
So the best way to
think about it is just

01:46.410 --> 01:49.790
figure out where its second
derivative has a sign change.

01:49.790 --> 01:53.450
And to think about that, we have
to find its second derivative.

01:53.450 --> 01:55.490
So let's write g of x over here.

01:55.490 --> 01:59.070
We know g of x is equal to
2x plus the definite integral

01:59.070 --> 02:02.572
from 0 to x of f of t dt.

02:02.572 --> 02:04.030
We've already taken
its derivative,

02:04.030 --> 02:05.010
but we'll do it again.

02:05.010 --> 02:10.430
g prime of x is equal to 2
plus-- fundamental theorem

02:10.430 --> 02:11.130
of calculus.

02:11.130 --> 02:14.920
The derivative of this right
over here is just f of x.

02:14.920 --> 02:17.950
And if we have the second
derivative of g-- g prime prime

02:17.950 --> 02:21.010
of x-- this is equal to--
derivative of 2 is just 0.

02:21.010 --> 02:25.090
And then derivative of
f of x is f prime of x.

02:25.090 --> 02:28.790
So asking where this has a
sign change, asking where

02:28.790 --> 02:30.820
our second derivative
has a sign change,

02:30.820 --> 02:35.180
is equivalent to asking where
does the first derivative of f

02:35.180 --> 02:38.610
have a sign change?

02:38.610 --> 02:41.230
And asking where the
first derivative of f

02:41.230 --> 02:44.540
has a sign change is
equivalent to saying

02:44.540 --> 02:47.980
where does the slope of
f have a sign change?

02:47.980 --> 02:52.900
You can view this as the slope
or the instantaneous slope

02:52.900 --> 02:53.490
of f.

02:53.490 --> 02:57.210
So we want to know when the
slope of f has a sign change.

02:57.210 --> 02:58.330
So let's think about.

02:58.330 --> 03:00.550
Over here, the
slope is positive.

03:00.550 --> 03:01.213
It's going up.

03:01.213 --> 03:02.432
It's up.

03:02.432 --> 03:03.890
It's increasing,
but it's positive.

03:03.890 --> 03:05.050
And that's what we care about.

03:05.050 --> 03:05.600
So let's write it.

03:05.600 --> 03:06.433
I'll do it in green.

03:06.433 --> 03:10.040
So the slope is positive
this entire time.

03:10.040 --> 03:10.780
It's increasing.

03:10.780 --> 03:11.260
It's increasing.

03:11.260 --> 03:11.843
It's positive.

03:11.843 --> 03:13.570
It's getting less positive now.

03:13.570 --> 03:16.820
It's starting to decrease, but
the slope is still positive.

03:16.820 --> 03:19.510
The slope is still
positive all the way

03:19.510 --> 03:20.882
until we get right over there.

03:20.882 --> 03:22.590
You can see it gets
pretty close to zero.

03:22.590 --> 03:24.220
And then the slope
gets a negative.

03:24.220 --> 03:26.320
And then right over here,
the slope is negative.

03:29.520 --> 03:31.530
The slope is negative
right over here.

03:31.530 --> 03:32.530
So this is interesting.

03:32.530 --> 03:36.140
Because even though f is
actually not differentiable

03:36.140 --> 03:38.960
right here-- so f is not
differentiable at that point

03:38.960 --> 03:39.669
right over there.

03:39.669 --> 03:42.251
And you could see it because the
slope goes pretty close to 0,

03:42.251 --> 03:43.850
and then it just
jumps to negative 3.

03:43.850 --> 03:47.700
So you have a discontinuity
of the derivative right

03:47.700 --> 03:50.430
over there, but we do
have a sign change.

03:50.430 --> 03:54.600
We go from having
a positive slope

03:54.600 --> 03:56.220
on this part of
the curve to having

03:56.220 --> 03:58.660
a negative slope over
this part of the curve.

03:58.660 --> 04:00.990
So we experience a sign
change right over here

04:00.990 --> 04:04.640
at x is equal to 0, a sign
change in the first derivative

04:04.640 --> 04:07.610
of f, which is the same
thing as saying a sign

04:07.610 --> 04:10.060
change in the second
derivative of g.

04:10.060 --> 04:12.650
And a sign change in the
second derivative of g

04:12.650 --> 04:19.970
tells us that, when x is
equal to 0, the graph of g

04:19.970 --> 04:22.860
has a point of inflection.
